INT. LAUNDROMAT - SUNDAY AFTERNOON

Yellow light. Eight dryers turning. A wall of mismatched plastic chairs, a cracked vending machine selling tiny detergent boxes.

PRIYA NAIR (40s, scrubs, hair in a clip) folds towels with military precision. Her son DEV (9, oversized hoodie) sits on a dryer, swinging his legs.

Beside him, a boy of fifteen slouches over a phone. He is a STRANGER to Priya. Headphones, buzz cut, one untied shoe.

DEV
Mom, he says he's my cousin.

PRIYA
You don't have a cousin here.

STRANGER
I'm Kofi. Aunt Lena's kid. From Accra.

Priya stops folding.

PRIYA
Lena's boy? You were this tall.

KOFI
Yeah. I grew.

Dev kicks the dryer door gently. A sock thumps inside.

An elderly woman, MRS. ABERNATHY (80s, cardigan, a cart of laundry), wheels past and nods at all three.

MRS. ABERNATHY
Lovely family.

PRIYA
(smiling, tired)
Thank you.

Kofi pulls off his headphones.

KOFI
Mom's going to call you tonight. She wants me to stay a week.

PRIYA
We'll make room.

Dev grins. The dryers keep turning.
